{"id": "d9471d67-d2c6-4c16-9cf7-58aa65ab0444", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "I'll run the read-only checks for these three resources and their connected workload components before I can tell you where you stand. Let me gather that now.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_ZuVguMe7HhuO4oONbthEo7", "tool_name": "gather_context", "input": {"prompt": "{
  "task": "Run read-only AWS checks to assess disaster-recovery readiness of a named workload consisting of three resources in eu-central-1: an RDS for MySQL instance named dr-demo-mysql, an ElastiCache (Valkey) resource named dr-demo-valkey, and a DynamoDB table named dr-demo-players. Return structured data only, no narrative conclusions or roadmap.",
  "context": "User wants a DR readiness assessment: what happens if the eu-central-1 Region fails, an AZ fails, one of these three services fails, or data is corrupted/deleted (bad data). They have no RPO/RTO targets yet. I (the calling agent) will write the final readiness report myself -- just return the raw findings as data rows.",
  "resources": ["dr-demo-mysql (RDS for MySQL, eu-central-1)", "dr-demo-valkey (ElastiCache Valkey, eu-central-1)", "dr-demo-players (DynamoDB table, eu-central-1)"],
  "timeframe": "current state, not historical",
  "criteria": {
    "step0_discovery": "First confirm each named resource actually exists in eu-central-1 (if any does not exist, say so and list closest name matches found instead of substituting). Then find the rest of the workload connected to these three resources by configuration: compute layer (EC2/ASG/ECS/EKS/Lambda) that connects to the MySQL instance, the Valkey cache, or the DynamoDB table; entry/routing layer (load balancers, API Gateway, CloudFront, Route 53 records) in front of that compute; any CloudFormation stack, tag group, or AppRegistry application linking them. If no compute/entry layer exists or cannot be found connected to these resources, say so explicitly rather than guessing.",
    "data_checks": "For dr-demo-mysql (RDS for MySQL): DescribeDBInstances for MultiAZ, BackupRetentionPeriod, ReadReplicaDBInstanceIdentifiers, DeletionProtection, StorageEncrypted, AvailabilityZone; DescribeDBInstanceAutomatedBackups for any backups replicated to another region; DescribeDBSnapshots for manual/automated snapshots and their region. For dr-demo-valkey (ElastiCache): DescribeReplicationGroups for number of member nodes/replicas, AutomaticFailover, MultiAZ, SnapshotRetentionLimit, GlobalReplicationGroupInfo, node type/family; DescribeSnapshots for existing backups. For dr-demo-players (DynamoDB): DescribeContinuousBackups for PointInTimeRecoveryStatus and RecoveryPeriodInDays; ListBackups for on-demand backups; DescribeTable for Replicas (global table) -- if DescribeTable is blocked, note that replica info could not be read and fall back to DescribeContinuousBackups/ListBackups.",
    "shared_dependencies": "Check whether a backup plan protects any of these resources: backup:ListBackupPlans, backup:ListProtectedResources, backup:ListRecoveryPointsByResource for each resource, and backup:DescribeRegionSettings to check ResourceTypeManagementPreference.DynamoDB (advanced features) since that gates cross-region copy support for DynamoDB. Check KMS: are these resources encrypted, and if so is there a key in any candidate recovery region (do not assume a recovery region -- just report key ARNs/regions). Check Secrets Manager for any secret tied to the MySQL instance (e.g. credentials) and whether it has replica regions. Check the VPC/network (subnets, AZs) the RDS instance and ElastiCache replication group sit in -- report number of AZs spanned.",
    "region_scope": "Only check eu-central-1 for the primary resources. Separately report, for each AWS service involved, whether any copy/snapshot/replica/backup already exists in any OTHER AWS region (this tells us Region-failure coverage) -- do not assume a specific recovery region, just report what you find.",
    "output_format": "One row per resource/component: layer | resource | type | Region | in-Region redundancy (AZs, nodes, tasks) | backups (kind, frequency, retention) | copies, images or IaC outside the Region | point-in-time recovery | replication | what could not be read. Flag clearly any call that was denied or skipped."
  }
}", "deep_research": false}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Survey dr-demo-mysql, dr-demo-valkey, dr-demo-players in eu-central-1 for DR readiness."}], "created_at": null}

{"id": "668ec2aa-e8af-4041-a454-7aee9c3093b6", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I'm finding good data now: the ECS cluster runs the dr-demo-api service with one task, and a Lambda consumer is triggered via an SQS events queue. I checked the three CloudFront distributions but none tie back to dr-demo - they're linked to other apps instead, so nothing there routes to this ECS service or API Gateway.

No API Gateway, Route53 zones, or load balancers appear connected to this workload either, so the ECS service seems to run without a front-end routing layer. I want to describe the ECS service and task definition to check its network configuration, security groups, and any environment variables pointing to database or cache endpoints, plus confirm whether the RDS and ElastiCache security groups permit traffic from the ECS tasks.

"}], "parent_id": "2b5a1b54-12f1-4e14-9271-21282eaa6a53"}
